 9098	/// Whether a declared anchor line contains anything a mutation can change
 9099	/// (M17.5 F-33 / blind pass 1 A04, A06, A10).
 9100	///
 9101	/// Operator rules already existed, but only over a mutation's before/after text
 9102	/// — i.e. only at stage 1b, where evidence exists. At 1a the packet's declared
 9103	/// anchors were checked for POSITION and never for whether the line held any
 9104	/// behaviour. Blind pass 1 walked in and found operators anchored to function
 9105	/// signatures and enum variants.
 9106	///
 9107	/// This rejects exactly one thing: a line that DECLARES rather than does. A
 9108	/// signature has no predicate to invert, no threshold to shift, no crash point
 9109	/// to disable, and no ordering to perturb; whatever the operator, the mutation
 9110	/// cannot be applied there, and a mutant that cannot be applied can never be
 9111	/// killed while still inflating the denominator §3 counts.
 9112	///
 9113	/// It deliberately does NOT try to match operators to line contents. A first
 9114	/// version did, and rejected three legitimate anchors: `NodeFlags(self.0 |
 9115	/// other.0)` is a real inversion site, `if rev == inner.state.head` is a real
 9116	/// staleness comparison, and `for entry in entries.flatten()` is a real
 9117	/// ordering site. Guessing which token an operator needs produces false
 9118	/// accusations against a mutation plan; "is this a declaration" does not.
 9119	/// Whether a mutant is GOOD is the 1b runner's question.
 9120	/// The aliases a `use` line introduces for the symbols it imports:
 9121	/// `use std::fs::{rename as mv, remove_file};` yields `("rename", "mv")`.
 9122	/// Blind-review follow-up (review of c455cdf): splitting the whole line on
 9123	/// `" as "` mangled a brace group into an alias like `mv, remove_file}`, which
 9124	/// matched nothing and reopened the evasion the alias scan closed.
 9125	/// `line` with a leading item visibility removed: `pub`, `pub(crate)`,
 9126	/// `pub(super)`, `pub(in crate::x)`. Unchanged when it carries none, and
 9127	/// unchanged for an identifier that merely starts with those letters.
 9128	fn without_visibility(line: &str) -> &str {
 9129	    let trimmed = line.trim();
 9130	    let Some(rest) = trimmed.strip_prefix("pub") else {
 9131	        return trimmed;
 9132	    };
 9133	    let rest = if let Some(open) = rest.strip_prefix('(') {
 9134	        match open.find(')') {
 9135	            Some(close) => &open[close + 1..],
 9136	            None => return trimmed,
 9137	        }
 9138	    } else {
 9139	        rest
 9140	    };
 9141	    if rest.starts_with(char::is_whitespace) {
 9142	        rest.trim_start()
 9143	    } else {
 9144	        trimmed
 9145	    }
 9146	}
